1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,877
For years, artificial intelligence suffered from a fatal psychological

2
00:00:03,927 --> 00:00:07,804
defect: it was physically incapable of pausing to think.

3
00:00:07,854 --> 00:00:12,167
In human psychology, Daniel Kahneman described two modes of thought.

4
00:00:12,217 --> 00:00:15,658
System 1 is fast, instinctive, and automatic—like recognizing

5
00:00:15,708 --> 00:00:19,148
a familiar face or dodging a falling cup.

6
00:00:19,198 --> 00:00:23,076
System 2 is slow, deliberate, and logical—like calculating seventeen

7
00:00:23,126 --> 00:00:27,003
times twenty-four or planning five moves ahead in chess.

8
00:00:27,053 --> 00:00:29,621
Every standard large language model—from GPT-3

9
00:00:29,671 --> 00:00:32,239
to GPT-4—operated purely as System 1.

10
00:00:32,289 --> 00:00:37,475
Because of their autoregressive architecture, every single token costs the exact same

11
00:00:37,525 --> 00:00:42,274
amount of compute: a single forward pass through the transformer layers.

12
00:00:42,324 --> 00:00:45,765
" and it spends a split second calculating.

13
00:00:45,815 --> 00:00:49,692
Ask it to prove a complex theorem in algebraic

14
00:00:49,742 --> 00:00:53,619
topology, and it spends the exact same split second.

15
00:00:53,669 --> 00:00:56,674
When humans try to solve impossible math

16
00:00:56,724 --> 00:00:59,292
problems in one breath, we blunder.

17
00:00:59,342 --> 00:01:03,219
And for a long time, so did our machines.

18
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:10,025
For a decade, the entire AI industry worshipped

19
00:01:10,075 --> 00:01:13,341
a single golden rule: the Chinchilla scaling laws.

20
00:01:13,391 --> 00:01:18,314
If you wanted a smarter model, you had to make the neural

21
00:01:18,364 --> 00:01:22,873
network bigger, collect more internet data, and run supercomputers for longer.

22
00:01:22,923 --> 00:01:26,188
But in 2024, researchers hit a breakthrough: pre-training

23
00:01:26,238 --> 00:01:29,504
was not the only place to scale compute.

24
00:01:29,554 --> 00:01:32,405
You could scale compute at test time.

25
00:01:32,455 --> 00:01:35,306
Instead of demanding an immediate answer, what

26
00:01:35,356 --> 00:01:38,207
if we pay the model to deliberate?

27
00:01:38,257 --> 00:01:44,010
What if we let the model generate thousands of internal tokens—exploring hypotheses, checking its

28
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:49,397
work, and discarding flawed deductions before showing a single word to the human?

29
00:01:49,447 --> 00:01:54,371
This was the core revelation behind OpenAI's o1 and modern reasoning models:

30
00:01:54,421 --> 00:01:58,930
accuracy on complex scientific reasoning doesn't just scale with training data.

31
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:02,660
It scales directly with the logarithm of thinking time.

32
00:02:02,710 --> 00:02:06,390
We discovered the second scaling law of artificial intelligence.

33
00:02:06,439 --> 00:02:09,311
When a reasoning model begins to work,

34
00:02:09,361 --> 00:02:11,815
something extraordinary happens behind the curtain.

35
00:02:11,865 --> 00:02:14,319
It opens a hidden internal scratchpad

36
00:02:14,369 --> 00:02:16,406
enclosed in special thought tokens.

37
00:02:16,456 --> 00:02:19,745
Here, the model carries out an internal monologue.

38
00:02:19,795 --> 00:02:23,084
It decomposes the user's request into atomic sub-goals.

39
00:02:23,134 --> 00:02:26,840
It translates ambiguous word problems into rigorous mathematical formalisms.

40
00:02:26,890 --> 00:02:30,179
It generates hypothetical test cases and checks whether

41
00:02:30,229 --> 00:02:33,517
its intermediate steps satisfy the laws of logic.

42
00:02:33,567 --> 00:02:37,274
" Through reinforcement learning, the model learned how to

43
00:02:37,324 --> 00:02:40,612
use this scratchpad as a dynamic working memory.

44
00:02:40,662 --> 00:02:48,125
If a line of reasoning leads to a mathematical impossibility, the model recognizes the dead end, crosses it

45
00:02:48,175 --> 00:02:55,637
out, and pivots to an alternative strategy—just like a human mathematician working through problems on a messy chalkboard.

46
00:03:03,199 --> 00:03:06,178
How do you teach a machine to

47
00:03:06,228 --> 00:03:09,207
reason soundly instead of hallucinating convincing nonsense?

48
00:03:09,257 --> 00:03:13,101
The secret lies in how we grade its work.

49
00:03:13,151 --> 00:03:15,697
In classic reinforcement learning, models were

50
00:03:15,747 --> 00:03:18,293
evaluated by an Outcome Reward Model.

51
00:03:18,343 --> 00:03:23,053
The model generated a full twenty-step proof, and the grader only

52
00:03:23,103 --> 00:03:27,380
checked if the final answer at the bottom was correct.

53
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:29,976
This created a dangerous trap known

54
00:03:30,026 --> 00:03:32,139
as the credit assignment problem.

55
00:03:32,189 --> 00:03:37,764
If the model made a lucky algebraic mistake that somehow canceled out and

56
00:03:37,814 --> 00:03:43,389
landed on the right answer, the outcome grader awarded top marks—reinforcing broken logic!

57
00:03:43,439 --> 00:03:47,716
To fix this, researchers introduced Process Reward Models, or PRMs.

58
00:03:47,766 --> 00:03:51,610
Instead of waiting for the end of the exam,

59
00:03:51,660 --> 00:03:55,504
a process reward model inspects every single deduction step.

60
00:03:55,554 --> 00:03:58,965
It awards credit for valid logical transitions and

61
00:03:59,015 --> 00:04:02,427
instantly penalizes logical fallacies the moment they occur.

62
00:04:02,477 --> 00:04:05,888
By grading the journey rather than just the

63
00:04:05,938 --> 00:04:09,349
destination, we trained models to become rigorous truth-seekers.

64
00:04:09,399 --> 00:04:14,241
For years, everyone assumed that to teach an AI to think

65
00:04:14,291 --> 00:04:19,133
like a human, you had to feed it human chain-of-thought demonstrations.

66
00:04:19,183 --> 00:04:22,246
Then came DeepSeek-R1, and shattered that assumption.

67
00:04:22,296 --> 00:04:27,582
The researchers took a raw base model called DeepSeek-R1-Zero and applied pure

68
00:04:27,632 --> 00:04:32,474
reinforcement learning using Group Relative Policy Optimization—with zero human demonstration data.

69
00:04:32,524 --> 00:04:36,032
The only feedback the model received was a

70
00:04:36,082 --> 00:04:39,145
rule-based verifier: did the computer code compile?

71
00:04:39,195 --> 00:04:42,258
Did the math answer match ground truth?

72
00:04:42,308 --> 00:04:44,926
Left completely on its own to

73
00:04:44,976 --> 00:04:47,149
maximize reward, something magical emerged.

74
00:04:47,199 --> 00:04:50,262
The model spontaneously discovered how to reason.

75
00:04:50,312 --> 00:04:54,709
It learned to allocate more thinking tokens to harder problems.

76
00:04:54,759 --> 00:04:57,822
It learned to double-check its own arithmetic.

77
00:04:57,872 --> 00:05:03,159
And most remarkably, it produced emergent self-reflection: spontaneously typing words like, "Wait,

78
00:05:03,209 --> 00:05:08,495
let me rethink this," and backtracking when it detected its own errors.

79
00:05:08,545 --> 00:05:12,498
Reasoning was not something we programmed into the machine.

80
00:05:12,548 --> 00:05:15,166
It was an emergent survival strategy

81
00:05:15,216 --> 00:05:17,389
discovered by the algorithm itself.

82
00:05:17,439 --> 00:05:22,304
We are witnessing the fusion of large language models with the

83
00:05:22,354 --> 00:05:26,772
algorithms that conquered Chess and Go: Monte Carlo Tree Search.

84
00:05:26,822 --> 00:05:30,793
Instead of predicting tokens along a single linear path,

85
00:05:30,843 --> 00:05:34,367
future intelligence navigates an expansive tree of thoughts.

86
00:05:34,417 --> 00:05:38,388
It samples alternative futures, scores each branch with process

87
00:05:38,438 --> 00:05:42,409
verifiers, prunes dead ends, and backtracks with mathematical certainty.

88
00:05:42,459 --> 00:05:45,089
Test-time compute has transformed artificial intelligence.

89
00:05:45,139 --> 00:05:47,770
We began with a raw autocomplete

90
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:50,450
engine predicting words on the internet.

91
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:56,258
We aligned it with human values, gave it external memory with RAG, and

92
00:05:56,308 --> 00:06:01,620
now, we have given it the ability to deliberate, self-correct, and reason.

93
00:06:01,670 --> 00:06:05,194
When machines can think before they speak, they

94
00:06:05,244 --> 00:06:08,321
cease to be mirrors of human text.

95
00:06:08,371 --> 00:06:11,895
They become engines of discovery—poised to solve the

96
00:06:11,945 --> 00:06:15,469
deepest unsolved mysteries of mathematics, physics, and science.
